      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gold Hill was founded in 1859 during the Colorado Gold Rush, becoming the first mining district in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Hill%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s oldest continuously operating public school, the two‑room Gold Hill School, opened in 1873 and still serves students today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Hill%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1930s mining boom brought tellurium to Gold Hill, linking the town’s history to a rare element that once powered early radio technology.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://goldhillhistorymuseum.com/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Bluebird Organization, active in the early 1900s, helped preserve Gold Hill’s historic buildings and foster community events that still echo today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://goldhillhistorymuseum.com/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Gold Hill’s historic wooden structures survived two major fires, and many have been restored, giving the town a preserved 19th‑century feel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Hill%2C_Colorado",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Perched at 8,300 feet above Left Hand Canyon, Gold Hill’s narrow dirt roads become impassable in winter unless vehicles have chains or four‑wheel drive.",
